  <h1>The Year of the Pause: How RBI Maneuvered its Policy in 2024</h1>
  <div><em>Economic Times | Date: Dec 18, 2024 • ~550 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>In 2024, the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) took an unusual but calculated stance: it maintained the repo rate at 6.5% throughout the year, refraining from either tightening or loosening. This “pause year” reflected a delicate balancing act — supporting growth while keeping inflation under control amid global volatility. For UPSC aspirants, this episode illustrates the complexities of monetary policy and central banking in a developing economy.
________________________________________
Why the Pause?
1.	Inflation Dynamics
o	Inflation hovered around 5%, within the RBI’s 2–6% tolerance band but still above its ideal 4%.
o	Volatility in food prices (tomatoes, pulses) complicated short-term forecasting.
2.	Growth Considerations
o	India’s GDP growth projected at 6.3% (IMF, 2024), fastest among major economies.
o	A rate hike risked stifling investment momentum; a cut risked overheating.
3.	Global Headwinds
o	The US Federal Reserve continued with high rates → risk of capital outflows.
o	Crude oil price spikes due to Middle East tensions threatened imported inflation.
4.	Liquidity Management
o	RBI used tools like Variable Rate Reverse Repo (VRRR) to absorb excess liquidity, while maintaining repo steady.
________________________________________
Implications of the Pause
•	Positive Outcomes
o	Stability encouraged investor confidence.
o	Borrowing costs remained predictable for businesses.
o	Supported government’s infrastructure lending push.
•	Concerns Raised
o	Household debt increased as retail loans surged.
o	Critics argued RBI underplayed rural distress and food inflation volatility.
o	Some economists feared “financial repression” by keeping real interest rates low.
________________________________________
Relevance for UPSC
•	GS-III (Economy):
o	Role of monetary policy in growth and inflation.
o	Repo rate, CRR, SLR — tools of RBI.
•	Prelims:
o	Functions of Monetary Policy Committee (MPC).
o	Target inflation band: 4% ± 2%.
•	Mains:
o	“Balancing growth with price stability.”
o	RBI’s independence vs government’s fiscal stance.
•	Essay:
o	“Monetary policy as an instrument of macroeconomic stability.”
________________________________________</p>
